<PageLayout
  title="FAQ"
  description="Frequently asked questions about using LLMs Unplugged resources, materials needed, and how the activities relate to modern AI."
>
  <h1>Frequently asked questions</h1>

  <img
    src="/assets/images/hero-faq.avif"
    alt="Hero image: Frequently asked questions"
  />

  <h2>Why do you keep saying LLMs&mdash;this is AI, right?</h2>

  <img
    src="/assets/images/headshot-ben-anu.avif"
    alt="Ben Swift"
    style="float: right; width: 8rem; margin-left: 1rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;"
  />

  <p>
    Honestly, the short answer (these days) is yes. The long answer is more more
    complicated&mdash;AI is a term that has been applied to many different
    algorithmic techniques and systems over the years. But today (in the
    mid-2020s) Large Language Models have kindof sucked all the air out of the
    room when it comes to AI, so that&rsquo;s mostly what we&rsquo;re talking
    about here. I (Ben) do like to use the term LLM rather than AI whenever I
    can, because I think it&rsquo;s more specific and accurate.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is this for?</h2>

  <p>
    Anyone curious about how language models work. The activities are designed
    for learners from high school age upwards, with no technical background
    required. <a href="/educators">Educators</a>, students, and
    <a href="/professionals">professionals</a> have all found value in the hands-on
    approach.
  </p>

  <h2>
    Did some of this stuff used to be called &ldquo;My First Language
    Model&rdquo;?
  </h2>

  <p>
    Yep, it sure did... and we still sometimes use that as a workshop title at
    the ANU. But this website and resources are for <em>all</em> the LLMs Unplugged
    resources, not just the ones we use in that particular workshop.
  </p>

  <h2>Do I need any special materials?</h2>

  <p>
    Nope, it&rsquo;s all stuff you&rsquo;ll have around the
    office/house/classroom. Each lesson has a &ldquo;you will need&rdquo;
    section.
  </p>

  <h2>Are there printable versions of the lessons?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes. We have a
    <a href="/assets/pdfs/lessons.pdf">combined lessons PDF</a> containing printable
    student handouts for all lessons. The web versions of lessons are more up-to-date
    and feature-rich, so we recommend using them when possible, but the PDF is handy
    for offline use or classroom printing.
  </p>

  <h2>Are there any video resources to accompany the lessons?</h2>

  <p>Not yet, but we&rsquo;re working on it&mdash;stay tuned.</p>

  <h2>
    You mention ChatGPT a lot, but Claude/Gemini/Deepseek/etc is more my jam
  </h2>

  <p>
    None of these resources are specific to ChatGPT (which is a brand these days
    rather than a specific model anyway). It&rsquo;s just a nice shorthand term
    for those who aren&rsquo;t so familiar with the term &ldquo;Large Language
    Model&rdquo; or LLM.
  </p>

  <h2>Can I use these materials in my school classroom?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes. All materials are released under
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/"
      >CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a
    >, so you can use, adapt, and share them for non-commercial educational
    purposes with attribution.
  </p>

  <h2>Can I use these materials in a paid workshop?</h2>

  <p>
    The NC (Non-Commercial) clause in our licence has
    <a href="https://www.artslaw.com.au/information-sheet/creative-commons"
      >some ambiguity</a
    >
    around paid educational activities. Our intention is that
    <a href="/educators">educators</a> at schools, universities, and non-profit organisations
    can use these materials in workshops that charge cost-recovery fees. However,
    for-profit companies cannot run paid workshops using these materials under the
    CC-BY-SA 4.0 license. If you&rsquo;re a commercial training provider interested
    in using these resources, please
    <a href="/about#get-in-touch">contact us</a>&mdash;we&rsquo;re happy to
    discuss alternative licensing arrangements.
  </p>

  <h2>
    I&rsquo;m a corporate client, and I&rsquo;d love to have some experts come
    and deliver this workshop for us
  </h2>

  <p>
    Good news&mdash;that&rsquo;s one of the things we do here at the
    <a href="https://cybernetics.anu.edu.au">School of Cybernetics</a>.
    <a href="mailto:lxconvenor.cybernetics@anu.edu.au">Drop us a line</a> and to discuss
    how we can make it happen.
  </p>

  <p>
    Honestly, this stuff is just as fun as a Christmas party/team bonding
    activity, but the learning is very real. Learning <em>can</em> be fun.
  </p>

  <h2>Why dice and buckets instead of computers?</h2>

  <p>
    Using physical randomness makes the probabilistic nature of text generation
    tangible. When you roll dice and look up words in a table, you&rsquo;re
    doing exactly what a computer does&mdash;just slower. This builds genuine
    understanding rather than treating AI as a black box.
  </p>

  <img
    src="/assets/images/workshop-4.avif"
    alt="Using dice to generate text from a hand-built language model"
  />

  <h2>How do these activities relate to ChatGPT and other LLMs?</h2>

  <p>
    Modern LLMs use the same fundamental principle: predict the next token based
    on context. The differences are scale (billions of parameters vs dozens) and
    learned vs hand-counted statistics. The core mechanism&mdash;weighted random
    selection based on patterns in training data&mdash;is identical.
  </p>

  <h2>Can I generate my own booklets from custom text?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes. You can <a href="/tools">generate booklets online</a> directly in your browser&mdash;no
    installation required. Just upload or paste your text, choose your options, and
    download a PDF. Your text is processed entirely in your browser and never sent
    to any server.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you prefer working offline or want to modify the tools, the
    <a href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/llms-unplugged">source code</a>
    includes a command-line tool that provides the same functionality.
  </p>

  <h2>Is there a research paper about LLMs Unplugged?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes. LLMs Unplugged was presented at the
    <a href="https://aceconference2026.github.io/"
      >28th Australasian Computing Education Conference (ACE 2026)</a
    > in Melbourne, Australia.
    <a href="/assets/pdfs/ace2026-llms-unplugged.pdf">Download the paper here</a
    >.
  </p>

